<h2>Why Parliament made reporting compulsory</h2>
<p>Child sexual abuse is overwhelmingly a crime of closed doors — committed by persons known to the child, inside families, schools and institutions whose instinct is silence. The POCSO scheme attacks that silence structurally: it removes the choice not to report. The duty arises not only on knowledge of a completed offence but on apprehension that one is <em>likely</em> to be committed, pushing intervention to before the harm. And by criminalising institutional failure at the level of those in charge, Section 21(2) makes looking away a personal legal risk for school principals, hospital administrators and employers, not merely an organisational lapse.</p>

<h2>The scheme at a glance</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Provision</th><th>Who</th><th>Obligation / Consequence</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 19(1)</td><td>Any person, including the child</td><td>Report apprehension or knowledge of an offence to the SJPU or local police.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 19(2)&ndash;(6)</td><td>Police receiving the report</td><td>Record in writing, ascribe an entry number, read over to the informant; arrange care and protection; inform the CWC and Special Court within 24 hours.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 20</td><td>Media, hotel, lodge, hospital, club, studio, photographic facility personnel</td><td>Report any material or object sexually exploitative of a child that comes to their notice.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 21(1)</td><td>Anyone failing to report or record</td><td>Imprisonment up to 6 months, fine, or both.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 21(2)</td><td>Person in charge of a company or institution</td><td>Failure to report an offence by a subordinate: imprisonment up to 1 year and fine.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 21(3)</td><td>The child</td><td>No punishment for the child&rsquo;s own failure to report.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 22</td><td>False complainants</td><td>Punishment for complaints made with intent to humiliate, extort, threaten or defame; children exempt. Good-faith reports protected under Section 19(7).</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The hard situations in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Doctors and hospitals.</strong> A physician treating a pregnant or injured minor whose condition indicates sexual activity confronts the duty directly: POCSO requires the report even where the family resists. The duty coexists with the obligation to treat and with the child&rsquo;s interest; what it displaces is silence, not care.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Schools.</strong> A complaint to a teacher triggers the school&rsquo;s duty — the principal who conducts a quiet internal inquiry instead of reporting assumes personal exposure under Section 21(2).</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Families in &ldquo;romantic&rdquo; cases.</strong> Parents discovering an adolescent relationship face an uncomfortable statutory reality: knowledge of what the Act defines as an offence engages the duty, and courts dealing with such prosecutions regularly see the reporting duty operate in tension with family judgment. The law as it stands makes no romantic-case exception to Section 19.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Institutions discovering old offences.</strong> The duty attaches on knowledge; discovering a past offence during an audit or inquiry does not excuse non-reporting because time has passed.</div>
</div>

<h2>Advising the reporter and the accused</h2>
<p>For persons and institutions on the reporting side, the practical rules are simple: report promptly to the local police or SJPU, in writing, keep the acknowledgement with its entry number, and let the statutory machinery — not an internal process — take over. Good faith is the shield, and contemporaneity is its proof. On the defence side, Section 21 charges added against family members, employers or institutional heads deserve discrete scrutiny: the prosecution must establish actual knowledge or apprehension at the relevant time, and the distinct ingredients of the reporting offence are too often treated as guilt by proximity to the main charge. Both perspectives meet at the same point — the reporting provisions are enforced seriously, and they are not technicalities.</p>
<div class="note">Reports under Section 19 can be made in any language the informant uses, and the police&rsquo;s recording obligations are designed so that illiteracy or distress does not defeat a complaint. The procedural framework around POCSO prosecutions now runs under the BNSS, 2023. This article is general information, not advice in any specific situation.</div>
